# FlashDrought
FlashDroughts

A Python package for detecting, analyzing, and visualizing flash drought events from climate data.

This repository provides tools for preprocessing climate variables, detecting flash drought onsets, and analyzing distributions of onset timing and severity using statistical methods such as kernel density estimation (KDE), linear regression residuals, and standardized drought indices.

Features

Data Handling

Works with weekly climate data (precipitation, potential evapotranspiration (PET), volumetric water content (VWC), etc.)

Supports rolling sums, seasonal subsets, and transformations.

Drought Detection

Implements flash drought onset detection using thresholds on standardized z-scores (SPI-like indices).

Allows onset categories (Abnormally Dry, Moderate Drought, Severe Drought, etc.).

Customizable detection criteria (e.g., must start at ≥ Abnormally Dry).

Statistical Analysis

Kernel Density Estimation (KDE) histograms for drought onset distributions.

Regression-based residual analysis for climate signals.

Pearson Type III distribution fitting for drought index standardization.

Visualization

Histogram and KDE plots of onset timing (by month, season, or distribution).

Regression diagnostics and residual trends.

Installation

Clone the repository and install dependencies:

git clone https://github.com/yourusername/FlashDroughts.git
cd FlashDroughts
pip install -r requirements.txt


Dependencies include:

pandas

numpy

matplotlib

scipy

sklearn

(You can adjust requirements.txt depending on the exact libraries you’ve been using.)

Usage

Example notebook setup (from your workflow):

%pip install FlashDroughts==1.2.2 -q

import sys
sys.path.append("..")
from src.FlashDroughts import KDEhistogram, DroughtDetection

Example Workflow

Load Data
Provide a weekly climate dataset with a datetime index and relevant columns (e.g., precip, PET, VWC).

import pandas as pd
df = pd.read_csv("example_weekly_data.csv", parse_dates=["date"], index_col="date")


Run Detection

detector = DroughtDetection(df)
droughts = detector.detect_onset(column="VWC", functions=["rolling_sum", "zscore"])


Analyze Results

kde = KDEhistogram(droughts)
kde.plot_distribution(column="VWC")


Check Onset Distributions

most_common = droughts.groupby(["State","County"])["month"].apply(lambda x: x.mode())

Methods

Z-Score Thresholds

≤ -2.05: Extreme Drought

≤ -1.645: Severe Drought

≤ -1.282: Moderate Drought

≤ -0.84: Abnormally Dry

≥ -0.5: Normal

Onset Detection Rules

Onsets must begin at ≥ Abnormally Dry (or higher).

Consecutive weeks tracked until conditions return to Normal.

Linear Regression Residuals

Regression of seasonal signals (e.g., PET trends).

Residuals used to evaluate anomalies.

Applications

Detect flash drought events across counties or regions.

Analyze seasonal onset patterns (spring, summer, fall).

Quantify changes in drought onset frequency and intensity.

Useful for climate variability and climate change research.
